<Base title="Privacy | GenATK" description="How the GenATK website and its private Search Console research integration handle limited data.">
  <article>
    <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
    <h1>Privacy at GenATK.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Effective August 26, 2026. GenATK is an early-stage informational website for the Generative Asset Toolkit.</p>

    <h2>Website visitors</h2>
    <p>The site does not offer accounts, contact forms, or payments. Unless the optional email-updates form is enabled, it does not intentionally collect names, email addresses, or other information that directly identifies visitors.</p>
    <p>Our hosting and security provider, Cloudflare, may process limited technical request information needed to deliver, secure, and operate the site, such as IP address, browser information, and request logs. GenATK does not use that information to identify individual visitors.</p>

    <h2>Search Console research integration</h2>
    <p>GenATK uses a private, read-only Google Search Console integration for the <code>genatk.com</code> property. It reads aggregate search-performance data such as queries, pages, impressions, clicks, click-through rate, and average position.</p>
    <p>The integration uses a Google account authorization limited to the <code>webmasters.readonly</code> permission. It cannot change Search Console settings, edit the website, access Google email, or publish content. Its credentials are stored as encrypted repository secrets and are not exposed to site visitors.</p>

    <h2>Optional email updates</h2>
    <p>If the email updates form is enabled, it sends the email address you enter directly to Buttondown, our email-list provider. We use the address only to send the GenATK updates you requested. Buttondown handles subscription confirmation and unsubscribe controls. We do not receive your password or payment data through this form.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>If GenATK adds accounts, forms, analytics, or other data collection, this notice will be updated before those features are used.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
